Z||000003||Fierowrecker||10-07-2001||09:03 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>Any opinions on the FBI's use of a large net device they are attaching to the ISP's 'core' to canvass emails and boards for information? It's called Carnivore, tho the name has been recently changed to appear less intrusive. This link will tell a little about it. Not very objectivly, but it's a place to start if you want more info. <A HREF="http://www.stopcarnivore.org/what_is_carnivore.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.stopcarnivore.org/what_is_carnivore.htm</A> <P>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 10-07-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They are the government, they can do as they damn well please...<BR>And don't you forget, we are just the peons that supply their war machines...<BR>We die, they survive...<BR>Only in America...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||10-07-2001||10:55 AM||||It's called a "sniffer". The technology has been around for at least ten years. Different parameters can be "sniffed", and other stuff can be filtered out. <BR>We could plug in to our network at the office and tell what traffic was coming from what computer. Could also read typed information, such a passwords, if I remember correctly. <P>Carnivore, if it indeed exists, is the Grand Wazoo of packet sniffers. <P>As an aside... I suspect that Echelon (if <I>that</I> exists) is similar. <BR>The only problem that I have with the Echelon concept is that it was supposed to be able to pick out <I>spoken</I> keywords. Sounds like BS to me. Unless there are repeatable digitized patterns that occur whenever a word is spoken. They would have to be looking for <I>that</I> word, spoken by <I>a particular</I> speaker. <BR>Anyway, back to the topic at hand...<P>The thing with carnivore is, if it's monitoring stuff as "broad" as an entire ISP, it's got to be extremely selective, or just plain <I>huge</I>. A single DS3 (44.7 Mb. 28 T1's) has the capacity to carry the equivalent of 672 individual phone calls or 56K modem connections. I'm guessing that each local POP of an ISP has way more than a DS3 of bandwidth. How many customers does Mindspring/Earthlink have? How many are logged on at any given moment?<BR>It's like looking for a needle in a haystack. <BR>Don't misunderstand. I'm not saying I like it, or agree with it. (I don't.) Just that they have tons of traffic to deal with if they are, in fact, looking at all of the traffic. I'm guessing that they are not. They have got to be filtering out most of it. They have much bigger fish to fry than the average net surfer. <BR>And they <I>can't</I> be reading all this stuff in "real time". The manpower requirements would be absolutely overwhelming. If they're <I>not</I> reading it in real time, then they are saving it to disk. It would take millions of gigabytes of disk space to store all this stuff for "future reference". <BR>Just my (long winded) $.02.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||10-07-2001||11:24 AM||||It is dubbed dcs-1000. According to wired.com's site, it is a special windows configured computer, that sits on a network and preconfigured to look for certain keywords or data. To read what it finds, an agent stops by and pulls a removable hard drive and takes it in to decipher. Supposedly, hotmail is a main target. I'm kinda against it's use for broad investigations, but needed right now for terrorists investigations. I think the FBI should use anything at their disposal in this instance. ACLU thinks differently. I don't think much of THAT organization anyway.<BR>They are involved in a fight to stop a school in Calif from displaying a certain sign in front of the school, relating to the recent surge of patiotism.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mbramble||10-07-2001||12:16 PM||||Read here to see how the FBI describes the way it works to a Congressional Committee on the Judiciary.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fbi.gov/congress/congress00/kerr090600.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fbi.gov/congress/congress00/kerr090600.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||DaRkLoRD||10-07-2001||12:33 PM||||Anyone want to guess what kind of computing hardware Intel is currently testing? Stuff we won't see for YEARS.. but I bet the government can get prototypes now... so what might seem like a daunting task by the hardware we understand, might not be too much for them.. plus they can cluster systems, process data a lot faster....<P>they are doing this. and I don't like it.. then again, I'm not a terrorist, so I haven't got anything to worry about.. right? Why should I care if the gov sees all the spam I get on my hotmail account? oohh.. or tracks my credit card usage.. wow, I buy lots of books.. tracks my web surfing? "hmm, I think this guy likes cars, graphic design and physics."<BR>It's just an uncomfortable feeling, knowing that someone might be watching whatever we do.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000008||mbramble||10-07-2001||01:39 PM||||Well according to what I read from the congressional briefing, Carnivore is not used to just monitor every piece of info that comes across the Internet.  Once the FBI obtains a search warrant, they would install their 'box' at an ISP, with the help and assistance from the ISP technicians.  Their box is fed only the stream of traffic that would possibly carry the "target's" traffic.  If it's e-mail they are after then they will only have access to the ports that e-mail flows thru on the machines that the target's email would be coming from or going to.  Then, carnivore pulls out only the information that the warrant allows -- maybe only the source and destination addresses off the e-mail, maybe also the subject, maybe the actual text of the e-mails to/from the target -- but only what is specified by the warrant.  No other person's e-mails or parts of the target's e-mails are recorded by carnivore.  If they grab more info than authorized it would probably make any collected information inadmissable in court, it would be a violation of U.S. law and whomever did it would be liable for presecution and/or being fired.  As of the congressional testimony in sep 2000, Carnivore had been used 25 times.<P>Of course such technology could be used for darker purposes.  But to do so would be in violation of U.S. laws and Constitution.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||10-07-2001||02:30 PM||||I have to assume that the CIA/NSA has the same tech... and from everything I've heard, they don't always act within the boundaries of the legal system or the american constitution. saying anything more would probably get me branded a "conspiracy nut".. anything else I say on this matter will be in PMs, if anyone wants to hear it.<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||10-07-2001||03:01 PM||||You all just notice this thing? Carniwhatever has been in use now for some time. It's not a sniffer by definition. It's sepcifally designed to work with/thru the mail servers to scan email to/from targeted individuals. The #1 problem is that no one knows how it works or exactly what it can do. The socalled independant testers who recently reported on it have a long history of working for the feds.<P>There is an alternate, open source i believe, system that can be used. The FBI has stated publicly they don't care what method is used as long as they can get the desired data.<P>You should be more concerned with Echelon technology wich is capable of analizing all internet and phone traffic on the tapped systems. There is no oversight of that system. Only the British have admitted that such a system is likely very real and in use.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||10-08-2001||12:07 AM||||If you think the NSA alone lacks the capability to analyse and then store text at a rate equal to the combined total global IP traffic on public space, then you've been living under a rock for quite a while  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Incidentally, if the NSA were listed under any public accountability, it would be a Fortun 500 conglomerate.  I belive it is *the* single largest employer in Marayland.<P>Intel? piff!  Try SUN, SGI, IBM, CRAY, DEC, etc supercomputers.  Lots of them.  More than enough to tap and mine information on a global scale, faster than realtime.   Hardly limited to the internet (infact, compared to teh telephone system, the internet is duck soup.)<P>I've probally said too much already, if I drop off the face of the earth in the next few days....<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
